1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
The unseen assassin that surrounds us.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:08,000
Over the centuries, it has slain millions.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
Its mysteries have been studied by shaman and scientist alike.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:18,000
And today, it may hold the key for curing some of mankind's most serious afflictions.

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Now, Poison on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,352
Tokyo, Japan, 1995. A noxious gas overcomes riders on the city's subway.

7
00:00:37,353 --> 00:00:38,680
Thousands are

8
00:00:38,681 --> 00:00:44,661
panicked, hundreds injured. Twelve people die. The culprit?

9
00:00:44,662 --> 00:00:47,500
A deadly poison called sarin

10
00:00:47,501 --> 00:00:51,100
has been released by a fanatical cult on the unsuspecting masses.

11
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:55,860
Serin is a, we call it a nerve agent,

12
00:00:56,560 --> 00:01:01,180
and it causes the body to basically drown in its own fluids.

13
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:04,320
People get sweating, they get diarrhea, vomiting,

14
00:01:04,620 --> 00:01:05,880
the lungs fill up with fluids,

15
00:01:06,380 --> 00:01:09,060
and generally death from a nerve agent is a respiratory death.

16
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:11,500
It's nasty, and it's very toxic.

17
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:14,480
Washington, D.C., 2001.

18
00:01:14,740 --> 00:01:18,020
The United States Senate building is rapidly evacuated.

19
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:20,660
There has been an attack on this austere chamber.

20
00:01:21,100 --> 00:01:28,380
The weapon this time, another lethal toxin, highly refined anthrax spores sent by mail into the building.

21
00:01:29,140 --> 00:01:37,120
Intolational anthrax leads to hemorrhage and then ultimately a respiratory illness that progresses to death.

22
00:01:37,121 --> 00:01:47,100
And the spores are so lethal that it's estimated it only takes maybe 25 to 50 spores of weapons-grade anthrax to kill you.

23
00:01:47,101 --> 00:01:58,318
In the midst of the heated campaign for president, opposition leader Viktor Yushinko falls mysteriously ill after dining with the head of his country's security

24
00:01:58,319 --> 00:01:59,860
Ukraine 2004. service.

25
00:02:00,580 --> 00:02:05,400
He suffers from excruciating back pain, pancreatitis, and paralysis.

26
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:12,680
Most obviously, the unknown illness ravages his face, making it almost unrecognizable.

27
00:02:12,681 --> 00:02:17,840
tests done in an Austrian clinic proved that Yushinko had been poisoned with

28
00:02:17,841 --> 00:02:23,480
dioxin a potent chemical carcinogen dioxin is a byproduct of herbicide

29
00:02:23,481 --> 00:02:29,600
manufacturing dioxin exposure can also cause kidney and liver damage even death

30
00:02:29,601 --> 00:02:37,580
although expected to survive Yushinko's future is as yet unknown in the 21st

31
00:02:37,581 --> 00:02:45,260
century, poisons still inspire fear and remain potent weapons. But as with these

32
00:02:45,261 --> 00:02:51,380
lethal examples, an invisible gas, microscopic spores, and a chemical waste

33
00:02:51,381 --> 00:02:56,720
product, they seem to come in countless forms. What is it then that constitutes a

34
00:02:56,721 --> 00:03:03,020
true poison? A poison is a substance that has an adverse effect on a human or an

35
00:03:03,021 --> 00:03:08,140
animal for that matter and it could be a variety of different types of substances.

36
00:03:08,141 --> 00:03:14,280
The poisons in nature come from usually the plant world, the animal world or the mineral

37
00:03:14,281 --> 00:03:14,981
world.

38
00:03:14,982 --> 00:03:21,460
So for plants we have such things as the digitalis, belladonna, even poison ivy.

39
00:03:21,461 --> 00:03:26,380
From the animal world we have things like the venomous creatures, the snakes, the spiders.

40
00:03:26,381 --> 00:03:30,060
We have stinging insects like bees and wasps and hornets.

41
00:03:30,980 --> 00:03:37,640
And in the mineral world, typically it's the elemental poisons like arsenic or thallium.

42
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:43,600
And then we have the chemical elements made by man, which include cyanide and many of the rodenticides.

43
00:03:44,160 --> 00:03:45,460
It could be a medication.

44
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,180
It could be a gas that's produced like carbon monoxide.

45
00:03:49,181 --> 00:03:51,980
The key that defines a poison is the dose.

46
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:55,080
At a certain dose, practically everything can be poisonous.

47
00:03:55,081 --> 00:04:00,140
Some toxins require very tiny amounts, such as botulinum toxin,

48
00:04:00,440 --> 00:04:04,480
the most toxic or poisonous substance known to man.

49
00:04:04,900 --> 00:04:07,680
Very, very minute amounts can be deadly.

50
00:04:08,240 --> 00:04:12,140
But in larger amounts, even table salt, for instance, is poisonous.

51
00:04:12,940 --> 00:04:18,100
The lethal actions of poisons can be as wide-ranging as the makeup of the poisons themselves.

52
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:24,520
Poisons can be ingested, they can be inhaled, they can be absorbed through the skin.

53
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:29,120
And once in the body, they can affect again different organs or systems.

54
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:32,980
If they work on a tissue level, if they cause destruction of the tissues,

55
00:04:33,480 --> 00:04:35,840
then you have effect on the organs.

56
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:39,580
Once the organs start to die, then the person can die.

57
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:42,820
So it's very specific as to the substance and how it acts.

58
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:46,680
Think of the poison molecule as a chemical monkey wrench.

59
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:48,820
We throw it into the human body.

60
00:04:48,821 --> 00:04:52,020
It begins to change all of the body chemistry.

61
00:04:52,220 --> 00:04:53,740
Therefore, it has the bad effect.

62
00:04:53,741 --> 00:04:58,740
The knowledge and use of poisons goes all the way back to prehistory.

63
00:04:58,741 --> 00:05:01,340
Boy, how did man first find the poison?

64
00:05:01,341 --> 00:05:05,740
I don't know, except it seems like it must have taken a long time.

65
00:05:05,741 --> 00:05:08,740
We have to go back several hundred thousand years.

66
00:05:08,741 --> 00:05:13,740
Well, ancient societies clearly relied upon the land.

67
00:05:13,741 --> 00:05:20,740
I think over the years through a lot of experimentation with what was tasty and what was foul tasting,

68
00:05:20,741 --> 00:05:27,720
one discovered problems with a variety of substances which caused adverse effects.

69
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:32,680
Our ancestors also witnessed the lethal power of poisons in the animal kingdom,

70
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:36,120
which must have inspired both great fear and curiosity.

71
00:05:37,180 --> 00:05:40,620
These toxins are found in the highly evolved fluid called venom.

72
00:05:41,400 --> 00:05:43,980
Venom is actually a difficult thing to define,

73
00:05:43,981 --> 00:05:49,340
but it's usually made up of a variety of interesting proteins and things

74
00:05:49,341 --> 00:05:52,860
that have different physiological effects on what these animals sting or bite.

75
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:57,240
To our ancestors, even the tiniest of venomous animals

76
00:05:57,241 --> 00:05:59,420
could deliver painful stings and bites.

77
00:06:00,320 --> 00:06:03,980
These are the arthropods, which include insects and spiders.

78
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:10,000
There's about 38,000 different kinds or species of spider in the world.

79
00:06:10,520 --> 00:06:14,680
Of those 38,000, there is anywhere from 30 to 40

80
00:06:14,681 --> 00:06:18,640
that are considered potentially dangerous by the World Health Organization.

81
00:06:19,340 --> 00:06:23,400
Unlike other arthropods, spiders deliver their venom through biting.

82
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,440
They use their mouth parts, which are actually modified appendages,

83
00:06:27,580 --> 00:06:31,980
which we refer to as chelicerae, but that are turned into sharp pointed things

84
00:06:31,981 --> 00:06:34,100
that we refer to commonly as fangs.

85
00:06:34,600 --> 00:06:38,060
The venom of a spider is produced by venom glands.

86
00:06:38,260 --> 00:06:42,880
These reservoirs and glands are closely associated with their fangs or their mouth parts

87
00:06:42,881 --> 00:06:44,040
just inside their head.

88
00:06:44,041 --> 00:06:48,340
When a spider does bite, venom flows through the hollowed out portion of the fang

89
00:06:48,341 --> 00:06:52,140
into the prey item, and it's as easy as five.

90
00:06:52,900 --> 00:06:55,600
The spider's close relation, the scorpion,

91
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,840
has a vastly different way of envenomating their prey.

92
00:06:59,540 --> 00:07:01,720
Scorpions do not inject their venom by biting.

93
00:07:01,880 --> 00:07:05,900
They use an additional structure at the end of a long tail called a telsin

94
00:07:05,901 --> 00:07:09,080
with a nice barb at the end, and we refer to it as a stinger.

95
00:07:09,480 --> 00:07:11,760
There's a poison sac associated with that stinger,

96
00:07:11,860 --> 00:07:15,440
and they use that tail to bend sort of around and over their body

97
00:07:15,441 --> 00:07:17,820
and sting their prey item.

98
00:07:18,340 --> 00:07:22,340
The scorpion sting has long been known for its potency.

99
00:07:22,341 --> 00:07:29,340
In 198 AD, the citizens of the city of Hatra used clay pots filled with scorpions

100
00:07:29,341 --> 00:07:33,340
and shot by catapult as weapons against the invading Roman forces.

101
00:07:33,341 --> 00:07:38,340
The Romans were driven away by this most unlikely of weapons.

102
00:07:38,341 --> 00:07:42,340
Another venomous arthropod is the bizarre looking centipede.

103
00:07:42,341 --> 00:07:46,340
Centipedes are all predatory and to help them catch their prey items,

104
00:07:46,341 --> 00:07:50,680
they have an associated venom and injection apparatus.

105
00:07:50,940 --> 00:07:56,120
And that injection apparatus, their stingers, are really modified front legs called calipeds.

106
00:07:56,240 --> 00:07:58,000
They hold them up very close to their head.

107
00:07:58,360 --> 00:08:02,860
People will refer to the attack of a centipede, if you will, as a centipede bite.

108
00:08:02,861 --> 00:08:07,480
However, technically speaking, the injection of venom from a centipede is not a bite, it's a sting,

109
00:08:07,580 --> 00:08:09,420
because it's not the mouthparts doing it.

110
00:08:09,680 --> 00:08:14,160
The arthropods most people associate with painful stings are of the hymenopteral order,

111
00:08:14,540 --> 00:08:16,260
which includes bees and wasps.

112
00:08:16,340 --> 00:08:23,140
When most people are thinking of bees and wasps, they're actually thinking of some of the very highly evolved forms of wasps.

113
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:30,340
The way that these wasps and bees inject their venom is with a stinger at the rear end, at the end of the abdomen,

114
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:34,120
and it's a small little structure that most people actually can't really see.

115
00:08:34,660 --> 00:08:40,060
The stinger appears here as a hair-like structure, protruding from the abdomen of this wingless wasp.

116
00:08:40,980 --> 00:08:44,000
And when they want a sting, out it comes and in it goes.

117
00:08:44,001 --> 00:08:49,160
While the power of these tiny creatures must have surprised and intrigued our ancestors,

118
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:55,120
they didn't have to look far to find even larger and more formidable venomous animals.

119
00:08:56,840 --> 00:09:01,960
Besides spiders, scorpions are the arthropods with the most human envenomations per year,

120
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:05,220
recording nearly 15,000 in 2003.

121
00:09:06,400 --> 00:09:09,280
Poison will return on Modern Marvels.

122
00:09:09,281 --> 00:09:16,500
One group of animals must have stood out to our ancestors

123
00:09:16,501 --> 00:09:20,960
because of their unique appearance as well as their lightning-fast strikes,

124
00:09:21,580 --> 00:09:22,280
the snakes.

125
00:09:22,840 --> 00:09:26,740
But in reality, only a fraction of the snake population is venomous.

126
00:09:27,940 --> 00:09:31,680
There are approximately 2,000 species of snakes throughout the world

127
00:09:31,681 --> 00:09:36,000
and approximately a quarter of those do have venom.

128
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:41,620
Snake venom, like that of other animals, is classified by the part of the body it attacks.

129
00:09:42,360 --> 00:09:48,460
Some snakes, like pit vipers, possess hemotoxic venom, which targets the circulatory and lymph systems.

130
00:09:49,160 --> 00:09:53,400
Most of the snakes that we see in North America are pit vipers,

131
00:09:53,720 --> 00:09:58,960
and they cause destruction of blood vessels, necrosis or dying of tissue locally.

132
00:09:58,961 --> 00:10:01,240
So you have lots of pain, lots of swelling,

133
00:10:01,241 --> 00:10:07,760
and what will happen is in some cases you have such internal bleeding to such an extent

134
00:10:07,761 --> 00:10:10,100
that your heart just can't keep up with the circulation.

135
00:10:10,860 --> 00:10:15,700
Another family of snakes, the elapids, possess lethal neurotoxic venom.

136
00:10:16,480 --> 00:10:19,060
This causes far less pain than hemotoxic venom,

137
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:22,240
but instead directly attacks the central nervous system.

138
00:10:22,980 --> 00:10:26,860
And you do not have symptoms sometimes up to about six hours after the bite,

139
00:10:26,861 --> 00:10:31,440
and all of a sudden you have big respiratory problems very fast.

140
00:10:31,800 --> 00:10:36,960
That would cause a fatality by basically shutting down your breathing and your heart.

141
00:10:37,560 --> 00:10:40,860
Knowledge of these venom types may have been the reason that this elapid,

142
00:10:41,320 --> 00:10:45,700
the African cobra, was used to perform one of history's most famous suicides.

143
00:10:46,240 --> 00:10:51,360
This is supposedly the asp that Cleopatra used to commit suicide with.

144
00:10:51,860 --> 00:10:56,180
If you're going to commit suicide by snake bite, this would not be a bad choice.

145
00:10:56,181 --> 00:10:59,360
and wouldn't be near as painful as some of the other guys.

146
00:11:00,040 --> 00:11:02,320
Regardless which type of poison they possess,

147
00:11:03,080 --> 00:11:06,740
all venomous snakes share a unique mechanism for injecting it into their victims.

148
00:11:07,640 --> 00:11:10,900
When a venomous reptile strikes its prey,

149
00:11:11,420 --> 00:11:13,440
what it does is by muscular contraction,

150
00:11:13,441 --> 00:11:16,440
it forces venom from the venom glands to the oviducts,

151
00:11:17,260 --> 00:11:19,740
to the fangs, out the orifice, and into the animal.

152
00:11:20,960 --> 00:11:22,900
Does the snake world have a poison champ?

153
00:11:23,560 --> 00:11:25,980
One species that is considered the most dangerous?

154
00:11:26,180 --> 00:11:27,546
There's several.

155
00:11:27,547 --> 00:11:36,080
There's a black mamba from Africa, which is a large lapid with short, fixed fangs, neurotoxic venom.

156
00:11:36,840 --> 00:11:41,620
Another very dangerous snake is called a taipan from Australia and New Guinea.

157
00:11:41,980 --> 00:11:45,480
Once again, it's a very large snake, which is about 14 feet in length.

158
00:11:45,760 --> 00:11:49,920
It has neurotoxic venom, and it's known for multiple bites.

159
00:11:49,921 --> 00:11:53,620
The fatality rate from a bite of these snakes is nearly 50 percent,

160
00:11:53,621 --> 00:11:58,900
and a fatal dose of their powerful neurotoxic venom is astonishingly small.

161
00:11:59,300 --> 00:12:03,960
With the Taipan, probably a very small amount, probably like 5 milligrams injected,

162
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:06,420
could prove fatal to a person.

163
00:12:06,900 --> 00:12:10,180
Along with snakes, there are other reptiles, like lizards,

164
00:12:10,500 --> 00:12:13,020
and amphibians that possess the deadly kiss of poison.

165
00:12:13,540 --> 00:12:16,700
There are only two types of venomous lizards in the world,

166
00:12:16,840 --> 00:12:20,720
and it's the Mexican bea lizard and the gila monster,

167
00:12:20,721 --> 00:12:24,480
which is a native to United States, which is found primarily in Arizona.

168
00:12:25,020 --> 00:12:30,160
The heel is mostly neurotoxic venom, can also produce an uncharacteristic amount of pain,

169
00:12:30,520 --> 00:12:32,220
and is delivered in a unique fashion.

170
00:12:32,860 --> 00:12:37,800
The venom and the venom apparatus of the venomous lizards are quite a bit different from snakes.

171
00:12:38,120 --> 00:12:43,000
They have saliva glands that have evolved into having some toxin,

172
00:12:43,520 --> 00:12:45,640
and these are located in the bottom of the jaw.

173
00:12:46,100 --> 00:12:50,500
And attached to these venom glands are some enlarged teeth.

174
00:12:50,720 --> 00:12:53,440
And these teeth actually have a groove on the side.

175
00:12:53,441 --> 00:12:56,540
And this venom and the saliva will stick into these grooves.

176
00:12:57,160 --> 00:12:59,100
And then the lizard will bite and chew.

177
00:12:59,980 --> 00:13:05,400
The most remarkable looking of the venomous amphibians are the brightly hued poison dart frogs.

178
00:13:06,340 --> 00:13:10,680
A lot of amphibians do have a very strong toxin throughout their body.

179
00:13:11,140 --> 00:13:17,020
In most cases, they just make the animal very bad tasting so an animal will let them go.

180
00:13:17,720 --> 00:13:23,340
It's evolved quite a bit to a more dangerous toxin in some of the animals,

181
00:13:23,460 --> 00:13:27,240
like little poison dart frogs, that are found in Central and South America.

182
00:13:27,860 --> 00:13:30,620
And these have extremely toxic venom.

183
00:13:31,260 --> 00:13:36,040
And these are actually the frogs that the natives use to tip their poison darts with.

184
00:13:36,041 --> 00:13:39,920
And this is a very strong neurotoxic secretion,

185
00:13:40,180 --> 00:13:44,020
and they will use it to shoot a monkey in a tree, for instance,

186
00:13:44,021 --> 00:13:47,700
and it'll knock that monkey right out of the tree in a couple minutes.

187
00:13:48,660 --> 00:13:52,180
Even when our ancestors entered the waters of the oceans and rivers,

188
00:13:52,640 --> 00:13:54,220
they came across venomous creatures.

189
00:13:55,940 --> 00:13:59,660
But most of these poison carriers are shy and rarely aggressive.

190
00:14:00,580 --> 00:14:04,300
They all have venom basically because they're protecting themselves.

191
00:14:04,940 --> 00:14:07,440
In most cases, they're very slow-moving.

192
00:14:08,020 --> 00:14:10,020
If a predator approaches them,

193
00:14:10,021 --> 00:14:13,840
they really don't have the speed to swim away and escape this predator.

194
00:14:14,020 --> 00:14:19,020
Most venomous fish are as unique looking as they are toxic.

195
00:14:19,021 --> 00:14:22,020
There are three primary groups of fishes that are venomous.

196
00:14:22,021 --> 00:14:28,020
And those are lionfishes, scorpionfishes, and the most venomous fish of all, the stonefish.

197
00:14:28,021 --> 00:14:35,020
In all of these groups of fishes, first 11 to 17 spines of that dorsal fin is where most

198
00:14:35,021 --> 00:14:36,020
of the venom is located.

199
00:14:36,021 --> 00:14:42,020
There are two grooves that run up the side of the spines, and each spine is covered with

200
00:14:42,021 --> 00:14:43,020
a thin sheath.

201
00:14:43,021 --> 00:14:48,700
When the spine is injected into the victim, basically the sheath is broken,

202
00:14:49,320 --> 00:14:55,640
and due to the pressure on that spine, the venom is released from the glands and into the victim.

203
00:14:56,240 --> 00:15:02,540
Like most painful hemotoxic venoms, an envenomation from any of these creatures will cause an immediate reaction.

204
00:15:03,100 --> 00:15:05,760
The venom actually causes a great deal of pain.

205
00:15:05,761 --> 00:15:14,380
In most cases, reported cases involving humans, it's the excruciating pain and the swelling that's associated with its venom.

206
00:15:14,660 --> 00:15:17,840
Other venomous sea dwellers actively seek out their prey.

207
00:15:18,200 --> 00:15:22,920
Sea snakes, they actually use the venom to capture and immobilize their prey.

208
00:15:24,360 --> 00:15:27,080
Sea snakes have long fired our imaginations.

209
00:15:27,940 --> 00:15:31,580
They are thought to be the real animals behind the ancient legends of sea serpents.

210
00:15:31,581 --> 00:15:39,920
real sea snakes inhabit warm tropical waters and are both more prevalent and more venomous than most people know

211
00:15:39,921 --> 00:15:48,460
well there are about 70 species of sea snakes and they're all venomous but all of the sea snakes are rear fanged

212
00:15:48,461 --> 00:15:57,000
so they have hollow fangs at the back of their jaw that basically they use to inject the venom into their prey

213
00:15:57,001 --> 00:16:01,060
sea snake venom is among the animal kingdom's most potent

214
00:16:01,061 --> 00:16:11,520
A full envenomation of a sea snake, which is very, very rare, can actually cause the fatality of three full-grown humans.

215
00:16:12,180 --> 00:16:14,700
So they are potentially fatal.

216
00:16:15,900 --> 00:16:20,340
The appearance of the graceful sea jellies belies their highly toxic venom.

217
00:16:20,960 --> 00:16:28,240
One thing that is found in all of these animals are stinging cells called nematocysts.

218
00:16:28,241 --> 00:16:35,440
And nematocysts are like small pockets that have a coiled up barb inside of them.

219
00:16:35,880 --> 00:16:41,320
And when the stinging cell, which is found in the tentacles of the sea jellies,

220
00:16:41,820 --> 00:16:46,580
comes in contact with a piece of plankton or a small fish,

221
00:16:46,740 --> 00:16:54,500
that contact actually releases the barb of the nematocyst and injects the venom into its prey.

222
00:16:54,501 --> 00:17:00,500
Our ancestors must have feared the many poison-carrying creatures they encountered on land and in the sea.

223
00:17:01,500 --> 00:17:06,300
But that was tempered by our desire to understand and harness the power of these toxins.

224
00:17:07,160 --> 00:17:08,280
And learn we did.

225
00:17:08,880 --> 00:17:14,140
For as we evolved into social beings, our knowledge and use of poisons also grew.

226
00:17:14,900 --> 00:17:17,100
Often with fatal results.

227
00:17:19,400 --> 00:17:23,840
The world's most venomous snake is a sea snake known as Hydrophus belchiri.

228
00:17:24,500 --> 00:17:29,940
whose venom is considered at least 100 times more potent than any of its land-based cousins.

229
00:17:30,840 --> 00:17:33,140
Poison will return on Modern Marvels.

230
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:40,600
No one knows how humans first came to learn about and utilize poisons.

231
00:17:41,540 --> 00:17:46,220
But it seems that as soon as we had acquired written language, we wrote about poisons.

232
00:17:46,880 --> 00:17:51,560
We find that the very first written records are with the Babylonians,

233
00:17:51,561 --> 00:17:54,920
where they're talking about a goddess named Gula,

234
00:17:55,600 --> 00:17:58,020
who is the mistress of charms and spells.

235
00:17:58,800 --> 00:18:03,240
If we move forward to ancient Egypt, we find a papyrus ebers.

236
00:18:03,760 --> 00:18:10,040
This is an Egyptian document which dates back to approximately 1500 BCE.

237
00:18:10,820 --> 00:18:14,380
And this puts together a list of substances

238
00:18:14,381 --> 00:18:18,220
that were thought to be poisonous during that day,

239
00:18:18,221 --> 00:18:24,600
including some plant extracts such as conitine and belladonna.

240
00:18:24,980 --> 00:18:28,720
Also some minerals are included like arsenic

241
00:18:28,721 --> 00:18:35,500
and also some animal poisons, particularly the venoms from snakes.

242
00:18:36,680 --> 00:18:40,920
A deep interest in poisons continued into the flourishing society of ancient Greece,

243
00:18:41,100 --> 00:18:43,660
which would give the study of poisons its name.

244
00:18:43,661 --> 00:18:54,840
The word tox, which forms toxicology, intoxication, etc., all comes from the Greek tox, which means arrow.

245
00:18:55,600 --> 00:19:03,920
And it's felt that it is tied in to the use by the ancient Greeks of poisons as arrow poisons.

246
00:19:04,520 --> 00:19:09,160
Socrates died after being administered a poison hemlock.

247
00:19:09,161 --> 00:19:14,060
and this was how the Greeks administered their capital punishment.

248
00:19:14,540 --> 00:19:19,980
Poison hemlock, or Conium maculatum, was a plant well known to the ancient Greeks.

249
00:19:20,740 --> 00:19:24,660
It contains dangerous alkaloids that affect the central nervous system.

250
00:19:25,420 --> 00:19:30,220
In the right concentration, symptoms like muscle weakness will start within 20 minutes.

251
00:19:30,960 --> 00:19:35,920
But it probably took Socrates a few hours to die when the hemlock finally stopped his heart.

252
00:19:36,820 --> 00:19:43,020
The Greeks and their contemporaries also had a desire to find something that could overcome a poison's deadly objective.

253
00:19:44,460 --> 00:19:45,880
Ah, Mithridates.

254
00:19:46,420 --> 00:19:49,180
There is a man who knew his toxicology.

255
00:19:49,760 --> 00:19:52,080
He was the king of Pontus.

256
00:19:52,560 --> 00:20:00,220
He took small doses of poisons himself every day, hoping that he would build up a resistance to them.

257
00:20:00,221 --> 00:20:04,100
He was one of the first to look for that universal antidote.

258
00:20:04,101 --> 00:20:10,580
But ultimately, he succumbed to the effects of some of these magical persons himself and died.

259
00:20:11,460 --> 00:20:17,400
It would be under another ancient empire that the trade of poisoning for profit first began to flourish.

260
00:20:17,800 --> 00:20:24,500
It is in ancient Rome that we begin to see a lot of murder happening at very high social circles.

261
00:20:24,800 --> 00:20:28,000
There were people who were actually hired to poison others.

262
00:20:28,280 --> 00:20:32,220
One of the most infamous examples was Locusta.

263
00:20:32,221 --> 00:20:36,260
Oh, Lacusta. She was Nero's favorite poisoner.

264
00:20:36,520 --> 00:20:44,960
Eventually, he was involved with her in the murder of his brother Britannicus, who was a competitor for the throne.

265
00:20:45,140 --> 00:20:53,060
So Lacusta was the secret ninja hitter of ancient Rome, and she was the master of all the knowledge of poisoning.

266
00:20:53,700 --> 00:21:00,620
Because of the rampant use of poisons, Roman aristocrats began a tradition that would continue for hundreds of years.

267
00:21:00,621 --> 00:21:01,567
food tasters.

268
00:21:01,568 --> 00:21:06,740
If these ill-fated servants lived after tasting the food, their masters

269
00:21:06,741 --> 00:21:07,922
could then indulge.

270
00:21:07,923 --> 00:21:12,340
With the fall of the Roman Empire and the onset of the Dark Ages, early

271
00:21:12,341 --> 00:21:15,943
scientific knowledge of poisons was virtually lost.

272
00:21:15,944 --> 00:21:18,840
But the advent of the Renaissance brought

273
00:21:18,841 --> 00:21:20,800
back the use of poisons with a vengeance.

274
00:21:22,020 --> 00:21:30,140
It was during this period that poisoning was rampant and poisoners for hire were very

275
00:21:30,141 --> 00:21:30,959
commonplace.

276
00:21:30,960 --> 00:21:35,940
In fact, in Venice in the 16th century, they had the so-called Council of

277
00:21:35,941 --> 00:21:41,520
Ten, who were essentially a syndicate offering their poisoning services to those who would

278
00:21:41,521 --> 00:21:44,008
hire them. And they would put out a price list.

279
00:21:44,009 --> 00:21:46,020
If you want to get rid of the Duke, it

280
00:21:46,021 --> 00:21:48,744
would cost you this much, or the Pope this much, etc.

281
00:21:48,745 --> 00:21:50,440
Well, some authors and historians

282
00:21:50,441 --> 00:21:54,880
have called this group the Italian school of poisoners. We also have other poisoners

283
00:21:54,881 --> 00:21:58,905
in Rome, like aqua tefana being sold by Madame Tefana.

284
00:21:58,906 --> 00:22:01,440
It was a water solution of arsenic

285
00:22:01,441 --> 00:22:06,060
who was being sold in small bottles, and she would sell it to women to go home and give

286
00:22:06,061 --> 00:22:07,549
to their husband with their dinner.

287
00:22:07,550 --> 00:22:09,760
And of course, then the husband would die, the woman

288
00:22:09,761 --> 00:22:12,296
would get the inheritance and move on.

289
00:22:12,297 --> 00:22:15,900
Madame Tefana made arsenic her favorite tool, probably

290
00:22:15,901 --> 00:22:18,662
because it was so difficult to detect.

291
00:22:18,663 --> 00:22:22,660
Arsenic is a real notorious and infamous poison because

292
00:22:22,661 --> 00:22:29,560
It has been used literally for hundreds of years as a poison of choice for someone who wanted to knock off another individual.

293
00:22:30,020 --> 00:22:35,320
Arsenic is tasteless and odorless and was really an ideal poison.

294
00:22:35,760 --> 00:22:37,360
Arsenic is an elemental metal.

295
00:22:38,220 --> 00:22:43,240
Mined from the earth, it has been used over the centuries in making ceramics, paint, and textiles.

296
00:22:44,100 --> 00:22:47,000
As a poison, it can present a confusing range of symptoms.

297
00:22:47,001 --> 00:22:54,240
And it's a very fascinating poison because it causes many different adverse effects to the body.

298
00:22:54,680 --> 00:23:03,400
It can cause a very vicious gastroenteritis or GI upset, similar to some infectious diseases such as cholera.

299
00:23:03,720 --> 00:23:10,140
But it can also affect the nervous system and can cause weakness and even paralysis.

300
00:23:10,920 --> 00:23:14,560
And this can be something that may prove fatal as well.

301
00:23:14,561 --> 00:23:19,660
The Renaissance also produced history's most notorious poison assassins.

302
00:23:20,280 --> 00:23:22,660
Among them, Lucrezia Borgia.

303
00:23:23,860 --> 00:23:28,200
Today, however, it seems that Lucrezia's evil reputation was merely a bad rap.

304
00:23:29,160 --> 00:23:31,420
Lucrezia Borgia never murdered anyone.

305
00:23:31,840 --> 00:23:38,720
The real killers in the family were Rodrigo Borgia, who happened to be Pope Alexander, and his son, Cesare Borgia.

306
00:23:39,120 --> 00:23:41,680
These were two guys that knew how to work with arsenic.

307
00:23:41,681 --> 00:23:46,000
You know, a lot of people would brag that they were going to have dinner tonight with the Borgias,

308
00:23:46,120 --> 00:23:49,360
but not many could say they had dinner last night with the Borgias.

309
00:23:49,660 --> 00:23:54,280
As Pope Alexander VI, Rodrigo Borgia continued a Vatican tradition

310
00:23:54,281 --> 00:23:57,460
wherein the papacy and fatal poisonings were linked.

311
00:23:58,240 --> 00:24:02,520
No fewer than six of his predecessors are suspected of falling victim to poisoning.

312
00:24:03,600 --> 00:24:10,180
During the Renaissance, of course, there was no ability to detect a poison in the body.

313
00:24:10,181 --> 00:24:17,880
Because of this fact, poisons were often employed to knock off other people for a variety of reasons.

314
00:24:18,520 --> 00:24:23,060
It would be the relative of another pope who would spread the practice of poisoning outside Italy.

315
00:24:24,040 --> 00:24:27,040
Catherine de' Medici was the niece of Pope Clement VII.

316
00:24:27,680 --> 00:24:35,040
She was the one who probably is credited with carrying the knowledge of poisoning from Italy to France

317
00:24:35,041 --> 00:24:37,480
when she married Henry II of France.

318
00:24:37,481 --> 00:24:48,440
In fact, everyone was so worried about her ability as a poisoner that he was given a rather large dowry, including a unicorn horn cup.

319
00:24:49,140 --> 00:24:54,980
During this time, it was believed that the horn of this mythical creature could neutralize any dangerous poison.

320
00:24:55,700 --> 00:25:05,060
And while Henry may have felt safer with his cup in hand, the knowledge Catherine carried with her spawned another poison school, this time in France.

321
00:25:05,061 --> 00:25:07,400
The French school of poisoners.

322
00:25:08,020 --> 00:25:10,820
And here we have some famous poisoners at work in Paris.

323
00:25:11,040 --> 00:25:14,440
Probably the biggest known one is the Marquioness de Brinvier,

324
00:25:14,760 --> 00:25:20,340
who killed her father, her brother, with her lover, in order to gain estates.

325
00:25:20,540 --> 00:25:24,780
And she was eventually found out and burned in Paris.

326
00:25:25,480 --> 00:25:29,820
By this time, knowledge of poisons included a large variety of plant poisons,

327
00:25:30,420 --> 00:25:32,180
especially poisonous mushrooms.

328
00:25:33,120 --> 00:25:36,200
This mushroom, the Amanita phalloides, or death cap,

329
00:25:36,480 --> 00:25:39,580
is among the most toxic and was probably used in this era.

330
00:25:40,480 --> 00:25:43,840
These mushrooms contain the chemicals Amanitin and phalloidin,

331
00:25:44,020 --> 00:25:45,680
which can destroy the liver and kidneys.

332
00:25:46,780 --> 00:25:52,280
Only one cap will bring about vomiting, extreme abdominal pain, and can prove fatal.

333
00:25:53,180 --> 00:25:59,320
The modern study of poison, toxicology, can point to one man and one book as its origin.

334
00:25:59,321 --> 00:26:04,280
There was a gentleman who went on to become what we call the father of toxicology.

335
00:26:04,640 --> 00:26:07,780
His name was Matthew Joseph Buonaventura Orfila.

336
00:26:08,160 --> 00:26:14,560
He wrote the first book in 1814 dedicated to his research in toxicology.

337
00:26:15,040 --> 00:26:22,240
The name of his book was Traite de Poison, which means in English, an exercise or dissertation upon poison.

338
00:26:22,720 --> 00:26:27,760
Using poisons like arsenic, opium, strong acids, and poisonous mushrooms,

339
00:26:27,761 --> 00:26:30,760
Orfila used a method unique to his time.

340
00:26:30,761 --> 00:26:32,760
He experimented on animals.

341
00:26:32,761 --> 00:26:35,760
So he would take arsenic, for example,

342
00:26:35,761 --> 00:26:40,760
and he would minister it to dogs and cats and rabbits and doves and pigeons and everything else.

343
00:26:40,761 --> 00:26:43,760
And he would dissect the animal.

344
00:26:43,761 --> 00:26:46,760
He would know what the observable signs inside looked like.

345
00:26:46,761 --> 00:26:48,760
That had never been done before.

346
00:26:48,761 --> 00:26:52,760
And really, his observations are just as valid today.

347
00:26:52,761 --> 00:26:57,760
By the late 1800s, America's had farms stretching from coast to coast.

348
00:26:57,761 --> 00:27:03,760
To keep their crops safe from rodents, farmers began using a poison, new to this country,

349
00:27:03,761 --> 00:27:05,760
that caused a fast and painful death.

350
00:27:05,761 --> 00:27:10,760
Strychnine is a compound that's isolated from a tree, actually.

351
00:27:10,761 --> 00:27:13,760
It's an alkaloidal compound, very bitter in taste.

352
00:27:13,761 --> 00:27:21,760
Strychnine is also a very interesting poison, which causes extreme stiffness and arching

353
00:27:21,761 --> 00:27:25,160
and arching of the back, it can interfere with breathing,

354
00:27:25,420 --> 00:27:28,960
it can cause muscle damage, and subsequently it can cause kidney damage.

355
00:27:29,240 --> 00:27:32,720
It is very toxic and certainly can kill other mammals very readily.

356
00:27:33,440 --> 00:27:34,880
By the turn of the century,

357
00:27:35,640 --> 00:27:39,100
poisons like strychnine and other toxins, such as cleaning solutions,

358
00:27:39,460 --> 00:27:40,800
were commonplace in homes.

359
00:27:41,480 --> 00:27:45,240
These were often kept in similar containers and in close proximity to medicines.

360
00:27:45,960 --> 00:27:49,280
The possibility of confusion caused an obvious problem.

361
00:27:49,281 --> 00:27:59,620
So some regulations were passed which said that these poison bottles had to have a way for the person to be able to tell that this was in fact a harmful substance.

362
00:27:59,621 --> 00:28:07,820
Multiple different types of bottles showed up, many with different shapes like triangular shapes or octagons.

363
00:28:08,320 --> 00:28:15,580
And many of them had raised ridges or knobs or quilted patterns so that when someone picks up that bottle in the middle of the night,

364
00:28:15,920 --> 00:28:20,480
they can tell by feel that that was a poison or a toxin not meant to be ingested.

365
00:28:21,800 --> 00:28:28,100
The outbreak of World War I brought with it a new and unprecedented use of toxins with horrific results.

366
00:28:28,101 --> 00:28:34,100
We probably saw our largest use of poisons in warfare during the First World War.

367
00:28:34,101 --> 00:28:37,100
And I don't know who fired the first shot.

368
00:28:37,101 --> 00:28:42,100
The Germans are credited with it at the Battle of Ypres in Belgium

369
00:28:42,101 --> 00:28:48,100
when they released chlorine gas on the Allied troops.

370
00:28:48,101 --> 00:28:53,100
Chlorine gas is a chemical that affects the lungs as well as the throat

371
00:28:53,101 --> 00:28:57,100
and ultimately leads to a drowning essentially.

372
00:28:57,101 --> 00:28:59,180
essentially. And pretty soon

373
00:28:59,181 --> 00:29:01,040
there was an escalation of chemical

374
00:29:01,041 --> 00:29:02,600
agents in warfare.

375
00:29:03,260 --> 00:29:04,860
And we have such things as chlorine,

376
00:29:05,520 --> 00:29:07,420
phosgene, mustard gas.

377
00:29:07,500 --> 00:29:09,000
They caused hundreds

378
00:29:09,001 --> 00:29:10,540
of thousands of casualties.

379
00:29:11,420 --> 00:29:12,340
It was terrible.

380
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:14,860
And while the Geneva Conventions of

381
00:29:14,861 --> 00:29:17,280
1925 prohibited poison

382
00:29:17,281 --> 00:29:19,120
gas from being used in battle during

383
00:29:19,121 --> 00:29:21,000
World War II, another

384
00:29:21,001 --> 00:29:22,980
toxic gas, which the Germans

385
00:29:22,981 --> 00:29:24,420
called Cyclone B,

386
00:29:25,000 --> 00:29:26,580
was instead used on civilians.

387
00:29:27,100 --> 00:29:28,400
We know it as cyanide.

388
00:29:28,980 --> 00:29:30,840
Cyanide is not easily found in it.

389
00:29:30,880 --> 00:29:36,060
It's not like you go to the cyanide mine and mine it like you do gold or arsenic or something like that.

390
00:29:36,120 --> 00:29:38,500
It's a compound that's created. It's an organic compound.

391
00:29:38,501 --> 00:29:47,960
It was used by the Nazis during the Second World War to kill Jews and others in the extermination camps in Germany.

392
00:29:48,440 --> 00:29:51,340
And they chose cyanide because it's so lethal.

393
00:29:51,341 --> 00:29:58,820
One can kill many people with a relatively small amount, particularly in an enclosed space.

394
00:29:59,200 --> 00:30:03,940
And when that gas is inhaled into your system, it's very rapidly acting.

395
00:30:04,620 --> 00:30:07,260
Generally, within about 30 seconds, you lose consciousness.

396
00:30:08,100 --> 00:30:11,600
Within about three to five minutes, you've stopped breathing.

397
00:30:12,120 --> 00:30:15,340
And in about five to eight minutes, your heart stops and you expire.

398
00:30:15,341 --> 00:30:21,620
A decade later, during the ensuing Cold War, toxic research was kept covert.

399
00:30:22,820 --> 00:30:27,780
However, poisons were studied at length by both sides for use as chemical and biological weapons.

400
00:30:28,860 --> 00:30:34,300
Perhaps the most notorious poisoning of the Cold War occurred on a bright spring day in 1978.

401
00:30:35,340 --> 00:30:42,180
We go to London, where a guy working for the BBC, but a defector from Bulgaria by the name of Gorgimarkov,

402
00:30:42,181 --> 00:30:47,440
on his way to work one morning, gets tapped in the back of the leg with an umbrella.

403
00:30:47,800 --> 00:30:52,980
Little did he know at that time that he'd actually been poisoned by one of the most deadly of chemicals.

404
00:30:53,640 --> 00:30:55,220
That chemical was ricin.

405
00:30:56,060 --> 00:30:59,000
Derived by a simple process from ordinary castor beans,

406
00:30:59,720 --> 00:31:03,360
ricin is a fearsome and as yet incurable toxin.

407
00:31:03,820 --> 00:31:09,900
Ricin can cause necrosis, cell death, damage to the organs

408
00:31:09,901 --> 00:31:12,840
like the liver, the kidneys, the spleen.

409
00:31:13,080 --> 00:31:15,440
It doesn't take much to cause death.

410
00:31:16,220 --> 00:31:20,720
Only after Markov's death did they detect that a tiny pellet containing ricin

411
00:31:20,721 --> 00:31:22,540
had been inserted into his leg.

412
00:31:23,600 --> 00:31:25,340
Poisons seemed to surround us.

413
00:31:25,760 --> 00:31:30,360
With a vast range of venomous animals, as well as the powerful man-made poisons,

414
00:31:30,361 --> 00:31:34,140
it was inevitable that science and medicine would form an alliance

415
00:31:34,141 --> 00:31:36,820
to control this deadly array.

416
00:31:36,821 --> 00:31:47,040
Nicotine, with a 45 milligram lethal dose, is actually more poisonous than arsenic, which has a lethal dose of 200 milligrams.

417
00:31:48,480 --> 00:31:51,180
Poison will return on Modern Marvels.

418
00:31:54,180 --> 00:31:59,160
During the 1940s, World War II raged across three continents.

419
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:06,240
The war also spawned many scientific innovations, some of which carried an unforeseen price tag.

420
00:32:06,241 --> 00:32:11,180
During the war, there was a real proliferation in the numbers of chemicals produced.

421
00:32:11,760 --> 00:32:15,100
There was the proliferation of pharmaceuticals that were produced.

422
00:32:15,640 --> 00:32:20,060
And it became increasingly difficult for a health care provider or physician

423
00:32:20,061 --> 00:32:25,380
to have much knowledge about this large number of chemicals and drugs.

424
00:32:25,900 --> 00:32:29,440
During this time, one man saw the need for a new agency

425
00:32:29,441 --> 00:32:33,020
dedicated to controlling the dangers of all these new toxins.

426
00:32:33,021 --> 00:32:39,833
The first Poison Control Center opened in Chicago in 1953 under the guidance of Dr.

427
00:32:39,834 --> 00:32:40,900
Edward Press.

428
00:32:42,160 --> 00:32:43,868
After World War II, Dr.

429
00:32:43,869 --> 00:32:51,960
Press found that there was a large number of childhood incidents that were the result of poisoning exposures.

430
00:32:52,140 --> 00:32:56,500
So a group of medical schools in the Chicago area got together and formed the first Poison Control Center.

431
00:32:56,501 --> 00:33:05,340
As the result of press's pioneering efforts, today, virtually every citizen of the United States has immediate access to a poison control center.

432
00:33:05,800 --> 00:33:11,148
Now we have 52 certified regional poison centers in America. Every state has access.

433
00:33:11,149 --> 00:33:13,440
They have the same telephone number.

434
00:33:14,320 --> 00:33:20,600
Poison control centers have now become the primary conduit of medical knowledge to people who have been exposed to toxins.

435
00:33:20,601 --> 00:33:25,600
The poison center is mainly an information repository.

436
00:33:25,601 --> 00:33:30,600
We have a phone line that's available 24 hours a day, seven days a week.

437
00:33:30,601 --> 00:33:31,600
Poison control.

438
00:33:31,601 --> 00:33:38,600
To manage questions from the general public, questions from physicians, emergency departments, and in their offices,

439
00:33:38,601 --> 00:33:41,600
handling specifically questions about toxic exposure.

440
00:33:41,601 --> 00:33:49,600
The calls received by the poison control centers have a diversity limited only by the numerous poisons people can come into contact with.

441
00:33:49,601 --> 00:33:50,301
with.

442
00:33:50,302 --> 00:33:56,360
In the home setting, you can find many toxic products unsecured under people's sinks,

443
00:33:56,860 --> 00:34:01,412
and their bathrooms, their laundry rooms, their storage buildings and rooms outside.

444
00:34:01,413 --> 00:34:02,113
Those can

445
00:34:02,114 --> 00:34:08,180
range from drain cleaners, floor cleaning products, furniture polishes with petroleum distillates,

446
00:34:08,720 --> 00:34:13,780
laundry products. Outside, we find car automotive products, wheel cleaners,

447
00:34:14,180 --> 00:34:14,880
antifreeze.

448
00:34:14,881 --> 00:34:19,380
We have herbicides, insecticides, many products which can be highly toxic.

449
00:34:19,600 --> 00:34:26,340
The sheer volume of calls received only underscores just how much the poison control center's hard work is needed.

450
00:34:27,740 --> 00:34:37,360
Nationally, the Association of Poison Control Centers last year received almost 2.4 million exposure calls collectively.

451
00:34:38,180 --> 00:34:41,580
A preponderance of those calls involves the most innocent of victims.

452
00:34:41,581 --> 00:34:46,580
A typical patient, 50% of the time, is heavily weighted on little kids that are into getting,

453
00:34:46,581 --> 00:34:50,580
you know, things they find left out on the counter or under the kitchen sink.

454
00:34:50,581 --> 00:34:57,580
Now, generally a child will ingest only a small amount of lick, taste, sip of any particular substance.

455
00:34:57,581 --> 00:35:02,580
That's not to say that there aren't substances that can cause significant injury in very small ingestions.

456
00:35:02,581 --> 00:35:07,580
Acid products, drain cleaners that contain corrosives,

457
00:35:07,581 --> 00:35:11,860
petroleum distillates that you can find in many products like furniture polishes,

458
00:35:12,400 --> 00:35:16,820
those are things that in even a smaller dose can cause very significant harm or damage.

459
00:35:17,720 --> 00:35:23,120
Despite the advances in poison control, very few actual poison antidotes are yet available.

460
00:35:23,400 --> 00:35:25,960
There's not antidotes to most poisons.

461
00:35:26,620 --> 00:35:32,460
In most of the ingestions and exposures that we see, the therapy is just very supportive care.

462
00:35:32,461 --> 00:35:37,240
There are only about five antidotes that are going to make a life and death difference,

463
00:35:37,241 --> 00:35:42,700
in that victim, and that's for carbon monoxide, for cyanide, for the opium kinds of products,

464
00:35:42,780 --> 00:35:48,840
narcotics. We have antidotes for toxic alcohols like antifreeze and methanol.

465
00:35:48,841 --> 00:35:50,020
We also have an

466
00:35:50,021 --> 00:35:53,118
antidote kit for the chemical nerve agents.

467
00:35:53,119 --> 00:35:56,360
But the greatest number of specific antidotes

468
00:35:56,361 --> 00:35:57,972
are for animal envenomation.

469
00:35:57,973 --> 00:36:01,600
These anti-venoms have been made in the same manner since their

470
00:36:01,601 --> 00:36:03,445
inception in the 1900s.

471
00:36:03,446 --> 00:36:08,500
The anti-venom is actually made from horse serum in most cases.

472
00:36:09,060 --> 00:36:14,900
And what they will do is they will pick a certain type of venom and they will inject a horse a

473
00:36:14,901 --> 00:36:18,241
little at a time and slowly build up that horse's immunity.

474
00:36:18,242 --> 00:36:20,280
Then they will go ahead and use that

475
00:36:20,281 --> 00:36:23,940
horse's blood as the basis for the anti-venim.

476
00:36:23,941 --> 00:36:27,680
New anti-venoms now being made from sheep serum

477
00:36:27,681 --> 00:36:33,920
are polyvalent, meaning that they can provide immunity from the venom of many different species at once.

478
00:36:34,400 --> 00:36:37,260
So they will inject several different types of venoms

479
00:36:37,261 --> 00:36:41,280
and build up immunity to those different types of snakes,

480
00:36:41,520 --> 00:36:43,520
and that's what will be injected into the person.

481
00:36:44,660 --> 00:36:48,840
The future of the poison control centers involves strengthening their current role

482
00:36:48,841 --> 00:36:51,160
as a repository of information about toxins,

483
00:36:51,720 --> 00:36:54,520
as well as finding better ways of dispensing what they know.

484
00:36:54,521 --> 00:37:01,040
I think our future will be solidified with appropriate funding and support

485
00:37:01,041 --> 00:37:03,860
to be able to continue to do the job that we're doing,

486
00:37:04,040 --> 00:37:07,400
keeping people out of the hospitals and hopefully keeping people safe.

487
00:37:09,500 --> 00:37:13,440
Toxicologists have always worked to free people from poison's deadly embrace.

488
00:37:14,320 --> 00:37:18,520
But now, researchers are asking whether these same lethal toxins

489
00:37:18,521 --> 00:37:23,320
may also prove to be the keys to unlocking the mysteries of our most dread diseases.

490
00:37:24,520 --> 00:37:27,520
even cancer

491
00:37:27,521 --> 00:37:33,520
in 2003 nearly two-thirds of all toxic exposure calls to poison control centers

492
00:37:33,521 --> 00:37:34,680
across the u.s.

493
00:37:34,681 --> 00:37:37,640
were for people nineteen years old and younger

494
00:37:37,641 --> 00:37:39,320
poison will return

495
00:37:39,321 --> 00:37:43,760
on modern marvels

496
00:37:43,761 --> 00:37:47,680
the road ahead in the realm of poison like the ancient god janice

497
00:37:47,681 --> 00:37:51,020
could have two very distinct faces

498
00:37:51,021 --> 00:37:55,180
is the chilling bio and chemical terror potential in this post-9-11 environment.

499
00:37:56,540 --> 00:38:00,900
Well, today it doesn't take very much knowledge,

500
00:38:00,901 --> 00:38:05,660
chemistry knowledge, to develop biological warfare agents

501
00:38:05,661 --> 00:38:12,940
such as anthrax or ricin or even chemical agents such as sarin, phosgene gases.

502
00:38:13,420 --> 00:38:18,240
And it's basically someone could mix some of these biological agent,

503
00:38:18,360 --> 00:38:20,820
warfare agents in their bathtub if they wanted to.

504
00:38:21,020 --> 00:38:24,520
In many cases, these are things that you can get your hands on every day.

505
00:38:25,020 --> 00:38:29,120
And they are often referred to because of that as the poor man's atom bomb.

506
00:38:30,280 --> 00:38:35,040
The first step in combating potential future bioterror threats has already begun.

507
00:38:35,780 --> 00:38:40,060
And the federal government, through an act of Congress called Project BioShield,

508
00:38:40,061 --> 00:38:44,300
is now beginning to fund research to try to develop vaccines.

509
00:38:44,520 --> 00:38:48,960
So now research dollars are going in to try and develop countermeasures

510
00:38:48,961 --> 00:38:54,500
against all these potential biological warfare or chemical warfare agents.

511
00:38:55,320 --> 00:38:58,980
But poisons have also recently gained another, more positive face.

512
00:38:59,960 --> 00:39:03,160
Science has found that some of the most deadly toxins, when controlled,

513
00:39:03,920 --> 00:39:05,760
can have remarkable healing powers.

514
00:39:06,480 --> 00:39:08,100
For example, botulism.

515
00:39:09,000 --> 00:39:14,260
Botulism is a toxin derived from a bacteria, the Clostridium botulinum.

516
00:39:14,261 --> 00:39:17,900
The reason why it's so concerning to the federal government

517
00:39:17,901 --> 00:39:22,380
is that it is the most potent lethal substance known to man

518
00:39:22,381 --> 00:39:27,580
in very small nanogram amounts

519
00:39:27,581 --> 00:39:29,940
can cause toxicity and death in a human.

520
00:39:30,360 --> 00:39:34,720
It stops the nerves from transmitting signals

521
00:39:34,721 --> 00:39:37,100
to our skeletal muscles so they don't contract.

522
00:39:37,480 --> 00:39:40,140
And this becomes important when you get to the muscles

523
00:39:40,141 --> 00:39:42,980
for breathing, the diaphragm.

524
00:39:43,380 --> 00:39:46,080
When that stops contracting, you don't breathe.

525
00:39:46,740 --> 00:39:50,480
And of course, when you don't breathe, that's not conducive to good living.

526
00:39:51,840 --> 00:39:55,700
But now the power of this most deadly of poisons has been harnessed.

527
00:39:55,960 --> 00:40:00,640
Botox is made from a type of bacteria called Clostridium botulinum.

528
00:40:01,260 --> 00:40:05,560
It's the same bacteria that causes botulism or food poisoning.

529
00:40:05,780 --> 00:40:10,440
The bacteria itself has been under scientific study since the late 1800s,

530
00:40:10,780 --> 00:40:14,460
when it was determined that people who got sick from eating poorly processed food

531
00:40:14,461 --> 00:40:17,020
were actually ingesting a powerful poison.

532
00:40:18,360 --> 00:40:24,980
In the 1950s, ophthalmologists became interested in using the toxin to treat eye muscle disorders.

533
00:40:25,560 --> 00:40:31,400
Word has it that a husband and wife team in Vancouver

534
00:40:31,401 --> 00:40:36,980
noticed that when the eye muscle problems were being treated,

535
00:40:38,080 --> 00:40:40,720
that as a side observation,

536
00:40:40,721 --> 00:40:44,940
that the wrinkling between the eyebrows seemed to reduce.

537
00:40:45,960 --> 00:40:52,580
And so the light bulb goes on that there may be some other usages for the botulinum toxin.

538
00:40:53,260 --> 00:40:58,440
In 2002, the FDA approved Botox to reduce wrinkling on the forehead.

539
00:40:58,441 --> 00:41:06,360
The bacteria grown in a confined laboratory setting is harvested.

540
00:41:06,361 --> 00:41:15,460
the bacteria is killed, and a small protein fraction of the wall of the bacteria is used to make Botox.

541
00:41:15,800 --> 00:41:23,060
Well, what we're trying to do with the effect of Botox is to have the small protein molecule in the injection

542
00:41:23,061 --> 00:41:27,360
interfere between the nerve and the muscle communication.

543
00:41:27,720 --> 00:41:29,760
It's very simple. You're cutting the telephone line.

544
00:41:30,760 --> 00:41:34,720
But Botox's future may involve more than just making wrinkles disappear.

545
00:41:34,721 --> 00:41:40,640
There have been reports in a number of specialties, and I've done with Botox for various reasons,

546
00:41:41,040 --> 00:41:48,780
all the way from muscle spasm in the neck to esophageal dysfunction in the GI tract.

547
00:41:48,920 --> 00:41:53,480
There's some urologic bladder prostate issues that can be treated with Botox.

548
00:41:53,680 --> 00:42:00,140
So I think the reports are well over 200 different arenas that Botox has function in.

549
00:42:00,141 --> 00:42:05,500
The prognosis for Botox in general is pretty much unending.

550
00:42:06,380 --> 00:42:11,720
Medical researchers are now looking closely at other poisons, especially animal venoms.

551
00:42:12,580 --> 00:42:16,120
Recently, venom from one species of snake, the southern copperhead,

552
00:42:16,200 --> 00:42:19,300
has shown remarkable abilities as a potential cancer fighter.

553
00:42:20,080 --> 00:42:24,040
Of all the many complex proteins found in this venom, one is unique.

554
00:42:25,320 --> 00:42:28,560
Snake venom is a mixture of maybe 50 or more proteins.

555
00:42:28,561 --> 00:42:35,240
Of those 50, this one protein, contortrostatin, has the activity as an anti-tumor agent.

556
00:42:35,840 --> 00:42:41,640
Making a logical leap, researchers began exploring this protein's anti-cancer properties.

557
00:42:42,260 --> 00:42:48,800
There had been some earlier work done by a group at a hospital in Philadelphia

558
00:42:48,801 --> 00:42:57,800
suggesting that there were, in snake venoms, some sort of component that inhibited platelet aggregation

559
00:42:57,801 --> 00:43:05,800
And since cancer cells have very similar proteins on their surface to those that are on the surface of platelets,

560
00:43:05,920 --> 00:43:10,820
we reason that, well, maybe this protein would have an effect on tumors,

561
00:43:11,400 --> 00:43:16,100
tumor spread, tumor invasion, tumor adhesion to surfaces.

562
00:43:16,480 --> 00:43:22,440
So we thought it might have both an anti-tumor and also an anti-angiogenic effect,

563
00:43:22,441 --> 00:43:25,340
and actually this is what we found out to be the case.

564
00:43:26,000 --> 00:43:30,300
So far, the studies have revealed that contortristatin has a dramatic potential.

565
00:43:30,980 --> 00:43:34,820
We've now looked at glioma, which is a very damaging brain tumor.

566
00:43:35,080 --> 00:43:39,100
We've looked at prostate cancer, we've looked at breast cancer, and we've looked at ovarian cancer.

567
00:43:39,360 --> 00:43:42,940
So in breast cancer and in the other cancers that I mentioned,

568
00:43:42,941 --> 00:43:50,960
the evidence is very strong that this protein has a very profound anti-tumor and anti-angiogenic effect.

569
00:43:50,961 --> 00:43:55,480
That is, it not only inhibits the tumor cell growth and spread,

570
00:43:55,600 --> 00:43:59,320
but it also directly affects newly growing blood vessel cells

571
00:43:59,321 --> 00:44:04,640
and blocks their ability to grow into the tumor and provide nourishment for the tumor.

572
00:44:05,840 --> 00:44:09,860
While this remarkable success has been limited strictly to animal studies,

573
00:44:10,640 --> 00:44:15,660
the hope is that soon these same results will translate into human clinical trials

574
00:44:15,661 --> 00:44:19,520
and that the use of animal venoms and other so-called toxins

575
00:44:19,521 --> 00:44:23,000
as treatments for mankind's most serious maladies

576
00:44:23,001 --> 00:44:26,460
will prove to be the side of poison's Janus face

577
00:44:26,461 --> 00:44:28,320
that will thrive in the future.

